{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017dp5tdVLkRwdPNRxVVRPPX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental temperature dependence Murday Cotts NMR cm2/s\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/?p=21016\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"subject:Self diffusion coefficients\",\"url\":\"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Pulsed field gradient magnetic resonance measurements of lithium-ion diffusion\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/104d2ab7-993c-4471-ba74-3523459e31b6\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion and isotopic effects in liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scilit.com/publications/a87ad4db2680c466d4fc33f00ce090a5\"},{\"title\":\"next.sciencegate.app\",\"url\":\"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517\"}]\n\nThe search didn't return the Murday & Cotts data itself, so I can't confirm their diffusion values or temperature fit. The results do point to the paper and to later work that compares against it.\n\n- **The original paper:** A reference list cites Murday and Cotts's \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in J. Chem. Phys. 48:4938–4945 (1968) (index 4-6, 4-7). This is the likely source for the NMR-based measurement you're asking about.\n- **Later comparison:** A 2012 molecular-dynamics study of liquid lithium reports that its calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also finds that the coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing, and it fits an Arrhenius equation to the simulation results (index 2-5).\n- **Isotope work:** A 1986 pseudopotential paper addresses self-diffusion in liquid lithium and explains the anomalous ratio between the self-diffusion coefficients of Li6 and Li7.\n\nFor the actual numerical coefficients and their temperature dependence in cm²/s, you'll need the 1968 Murday & Cotts paper itself (J. Chem. Phys. 48, 4938), which this search did not surface. Pulling up that paper or a review that tabulates its Arrhenius fit would be the next step.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c452f78-34bc-4273-98ea-540346e2ed75","uuid":"553c2d06-f86f-4841-b880-bb9f769657ce","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:37:13.455Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental temperature dependence Murday Cotts NMR cm2/s","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QYCrUwD6o41eauFZEBxNTR","content":[{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/?p=21016"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"subject:Self diffusion coefficients","url":"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/d3/cp/d3cp04666d/d3cp04666d1.pdf"},{"title":"Pulsed field gradient magnetic resonance measurements of lithium-ion diffusion","url":"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/104d2ab7-993c-4471-ba74-3523459e31b6"},{"title":"Self-diffusion and isotopic effects in liquid lithium","url":"https://scilit.com/publications/a87ad4db2680c466d4fc33f00ce090a5"},{"title":"next.sciencegate.app","url":"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517"}]},"The search didn't return the Murday & Cotts data itself, so I can't confirm their diffusion values or temperature fit. The results do point to the paper and to later work that compares against it.\n\n- **The original paper:** A reference list cites Murday and Cotts's \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in J. Chem. Phys. 48:4938–4945 (1968) (index 4-6, 4-7). This is the likely source for the NMR-based measurement you're asking about.\n- **Later comparison:** A 2012 molecular-dynamics study of liquid lithium reports that its calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere. It also finds that the coefficient increases with temperature increasing and decreases with pressure increasing, and it fits an Arrhenius equation to the simulation results (index 2-5).\n- **Isotope work:** A 1986 pseudopotential paper addresses self-diffusion in liquid lithium and explains the anomalous ratio between the self-diffusion coefficients of Li6 and Li7.\n\nFor the actual numerical coefficients and their temperature dependence in cm²/s, you'll need the 1968 Murday & Cotts paper itself (J. Chem. Phys. 48, 4938), which this search did not surface. Pulling up that paper or a review that tabulates its Arrhenius fit would be the next step."],"durationSeconds":4.949894138999996,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017dp5tdVLkRwdPNRxVVRPPX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
